WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.800
Smell and taste both detect chemicals.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.780
Why do they read them so differently?

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.100
In a mouse, most mature olfactory neurons express one receptor gene at high

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.560
abundance, chosen from roughly a thousand.

5
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.240
That is a statement about genes, not about odorants.

6
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.200
One receptor can still bind many odorants.

7
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:24.553
Where do their axons go?

8
00:00:25.073 --> 00:00:29.973
In mice, axons from neurons that share one receptor start scattered across the

9
00:00:29.973 --> 00:00:31.273
olfactory epithelium.

10
00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:35.573
They converge on two fixed glomeruli in each olfactory bulb.

11
00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:39.393
So each glomerulus collects mainly from one receptor type.

12
00:00:39.393 --> 00:00:43.393
The glomerular layer is largely a map of receptor identity.

13
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:50.007
Here is an invented example with four receptors, A to D. Odorant two activates B and

14
00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:55.827
C. Odorant three activates A, B and C. Imaging of mouse olfactory neurons found the

15
00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:59.627
same two things. One receptor recognized several odorants.

16
00:01:00.247 --> 00:01:02.767
One odorant was recognized by several receptors.

17
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:09.190
Now remove receptor A. Odorant two still gives B and C. Odorant three now gives B

18
00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:13.110
and C as well. The model predicts they can no longer be told apart.

19
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:19.090
Odorant one activates A and B. At tenfold concentration it also recruits receptor C,

20
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:20.710
a lower-affinity receptor.

21
00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.350
Its pattern now matches odorant three.

22
00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.610
The model predicts it could be mistaken for odorant three.

23
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:29.530
This is a toy model, not a count of smells.

24
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:31.953
Taste works differently.

25
00:01:32.553 --> 00:01:36.573
In mouse taste buds, sweet, umami and bitter cells carry different receptors.

26
00:01:37.373 --> 00:01:41.833
After the receptor, they share the same steps, including the enzyme PLCβ2.

27
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:45.133
If the machinery is shared, what keeps sweet and bitter apart?

28
00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:48.193
Zhang and colleagues removed PLCβ2.

29
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:52.333
The tested sweet, amino acid and bitter responses were lost.

30
00:01:53.293 --> 00:01:55.033
Sour and salty responses were spared.

31
00:01:56.033 --> 00:02:00.673
Then they restored PLCβ2 only in cells that express bitter receptors.

32
00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:02.793
Bitter responses returned.

33
00:02:03.513 --> 00:02:05.973
Sweet and amino acid responses did not.

34
00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:10.393
Bitter runs in its own cells, wired to report bitter and not sweet.

35
00:02:10.993 --> 00:02:12.833
These are mouse experiments on the periphery.

36
00:02:13.753 --> 00:02:14.753
Smell reads a pattern.

37
00:02:15.293 --> 00:02:17.053
Taste mostly reads which cell fires.
